// =========================================================================
// Title: Still Image  (STILL image on a strip)
//
// PURPOSE: the template the other presets in this folder are built on, with
// the IDENTITY matrix (no motion). It resamples the image of the strip it is
// applied to -- or, if the strip has no image of its own, the surface
// image -- with zoom and shift adjustable below.
//
// HOW TO USE:
//   1. select the mesh and pick an image (Library > Textures > Images):
//      it goes onto the selected strip only;
//   2. apply this script to the same strip.
// In "All" scope the image and the script go onto the whole surface.
//
// NO ANIMATION: the transformation is the IDENTITY matrix, which leaves the
// coordinates as they are. To animate the image use the other presets in
// this folder (Drifting / Rotating / Breathing / Shearing), all built on
// this same scheme with a different 2x2 matrix.
//
// TWO CONSTRAINTS OF THIS ENGINE'S SHADERTOY BRANCH:
//  1. the A..F sliders and t can NOT be used here: the engine declares them
//     as LOCAL variables of getCustomColor(), while mainImage() is a
//     separate function -> "'A' : undeclared identifier";
//  2. no MODULE-level declarations (const, functions, #define): with two
//     meshes using this script the fragment would contain two copies of
//     them -> "redefinition", and the surface would disappear.
// That is why the parameters live INSIDE mainImage.
// =========================================================================

void mainImage(out vec4 Q, vec2 U)
{
    // --- PARAMETERS (edit here) ---
    float zoom = 1.0;     // >1 enlarges, <1 shrinks
    vec2  shift = vec2(0.0, 0.0);   // shift: x to the right, y up

    // Centred coordinate: in the Shadertoy branch iResolution is 1024,
    // so V runs roughly over [-0.5, 0.5].
    vec2 V = U / iResolution.xy - 0.5;

    // IDENTITY matrix: no deformation, no rotation.
    // This is the place for another 2x2 matrix to get an effect:
    // just replace these two lines.
    mat2 M = mat2(1.0, 0.0,
                  0.0, 1.0);
    V = M * V;

    // Framing. Divide by zoom because shrinking the COORDINATES
    // enlarges the image (they are inverse).
    V = V / zoom - shift;

    // Direct sampling, no mip bias: the image stays sharp.
    // +0.5 brings the coordinate back to [0,1].
    Q = texture(iChannel0, V + 0.5);
}
